# Inline WireGuard hooks for SPLIT-TUNNEL WARP — paste into the [Interface]
# section of /etc/wireguard/wgcf.conf. See ../../README.md for the why.
#
# Model: the host default route stays on the WAN. We send ONLY user traffic
# (Docker proxies, Tailscale exit-node, your own WireGuard subnet) into a
# dedicated WARP table. Because WARP is never the default route, there is no
# inbound-reply problem, no conntrack marking, no Tailscale carve-out, no
# rp_filter requirement, and SSH/host traffic is never at risk.
#
# Why inline (not a script): Ubuntu's enforced AppArmor profile for wg-quick
# blocks executing scripts in /usr/local/bin, but allows iptables/ip/sysctl.
#
# Before using:
#   - Remove the `DNS =` line from wgcf.conf (its teardown can be AppArmor-denied
#     on systemd-resolved hosts, which aborts cleanup and locks you out).
#   - `Table = off` is REQUIRED: it tells wg-quick to touch no routes or rules,
#     so the WAN default route (and the wgcf handshake itself) stay on the main
#     table. We add everything explicitly below. `%i` = the tunnel interface.
#   - Adjust the source selectors to match your setup:
#       * 172.16.0.0/12      covers all Docker bridge networks (default).
#       * 10.x.x.0/24         your own WireGuard client subnet, if you run one.
#                             Delete those lines if you don't.
#   - Tailscale exit node? See the OPTIONAL block at the bottom of this file.

Table = off

# --- Route the WARP table out the tunnel -------------------------------------
PostUp = ip route add default dev %i table 51820
PostUp = ip -6 route add default dev %i table 51820

# --- Opt user traffic INTO the WARP table ------------------------------------
PostUp = ip rule add from 172.16.0.0/12 table 51820 pref 100
# PostUp = ip rule add from 10.13.13.0/24 table 51820 pref 102
# IPv6 Docker/WireGuard subnets: add `ip -6 rule add from <prefix> ...` here if
# you hand IPv6 to clients. If you do NOT, disable IPv6 on the host so clients
# cannot leak straight out the server's real IPv6 (bypassing WARP).

# --- NAT user traffic onto the tunnel ----------------------------------------
PostUp = iptables  -t nat -A POSTROUTING -o %i -j MASQUERADE
PostUp = ip6tables -t nat -A POSTROUTING -o %i -j MASQUERADE

# --- Teardown (|| true so a missing rule never aborts cleanup) ----------------
PostDown = iptables  -t nat -D POSTROUTING -o %i -j MASQUERADE || true
PostDown = ip6tables -t nat -D POSTROUTING -o %i -j MASQUERADE || true
PostDown = ip rule del from 172.16.0.0/12 table 51820 pref 100 || true
# PostDown = ip rule del from 10.13.13.0/24 table 51820 pref 102 || true
PostDown = ip route flush table 51820 || true
PostDown = ip -6 route flush table 51820 || true

# --- OPTIONAL: Tailscale exit node -------------------------------------------
# Only if this box is a Tailscale exit node. Opts traffic forwarded from the
# tailnet INTO WARP (matched by inbound interface, before Tailscale's
# masquerade). Delete this block entirely if you don't run Tailscale.
# PostUp = ip rule add iif tailscale0 table 51820 pref 101
# PostUp = ip -6 rule add iif tailscale0 table 51820 pref 101
# PostDown = ip rule del iif tailscale0 table 51820 pref 101 || true
# PostDown = ip -6 rule del iif tailscale0 table 51820 pref 101 || true
